{"excerpt": "[6c4abc40a33f] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Idempotent and safe are distinct: safe means read-only (no server state change), while idempotent means state may change but identical repeated requests yield the same result as a single one. An append-only event log surviving at-least-once delivery requires writers to be idempotent, not safe, because writers must modify state yet retries must not compound.\nMECHANISM: At-least-once delivery retries writes that may have already succeeded. If the write were merely safe it could not record an event at all (safe = no state mutation). If the write were non-idempotent, each retry would append a duplicate event, corrupting replay determinism. The causal mechanism is deduplication: the writer attaches a stable operation key (or sequence number) so the log's append path can detect a replayed entry and discard it, collapsing the effect of N retries to the e", "handle": "ART_1", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
{"excerpt": "[7e25700ea502] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Safe is a strictly stronger property than idempotent in the sense that all safe methods are idempotent (repeating a no-op yields a no-op), but the converse fails. An append-only event log needs the weaker property — idempotent writers — because it must tolerate side effects (writing events) while bounding them under retry.\nMECHANISM: RFC 9110 classifies safe methods as idempotent because a read-only operation repeated produces no additional state change. The hierarchy is: safe ⇒ idempotent, but idempotent ⇏ safe (PUT is idempotent but not safe). The log's writers occupy the idempotent-but-not-safe cell: they mutate the log, but their mutation is repeatable. The mechanism is conditional append keyed by a content hash or client-side idempotency token; the log stores a fingerprint of prior writes and rejects duplicates at the append boundary. This is", "handle": "ART_2", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
