{"excerpt": "[619b760e99d7] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: The append-only event log requires safe operations from its readers and idempotent operations from its writers: the asymmetry is structural. Writers mutate (so not safe) but must be repeatable (idempotent); readers must not mutate (safe) and therefore are trivially idempotent.\nMECHANISM: At-least-once delivery applies to both read and write paths in distributed logs. On the write path, idempotence via deduplication keys is the minimal requirement because safety would forbid writes. On the read path, safety suffices and implies idempotence for free. The causal mechanism is the split of the log protocol into a mutation channel (writer→log, idempotent) and an observation channel (reader←log, safe). This maps to RFC 9110's separation of methods into those that change state (PUT, POST, DELETE) and those that don't (GET, HEAD, OPTIONS).", "handle": "ART_4", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
{"excerpt": "[748e0b32303a] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: Per RFC 9110, a safe method is one whose semantics are essentially read-only (no server state change is intended or expected), while an idempotent method is one where multiple identical requests have the same intended server effect as a single request. The distinction is that safe forbids side effects entirely, whereas idempotent permits side effects but requires them to converge. An append-only event log with at-least-once delivery requires idempotence of its writers — not safety — because appending an event is inherently state-changing (not read-only), yet a retried append must not duplicate the event.\nMECHANISM: Safety constrains the effect class (no mutation). Idempotence constrains the effect's behavior under repetition (mutation is allowed but must be invariant under duplication). In an append-only log, writers mutate state (append), so safe", "handle": "ART_5", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
{"excerpt": "[08240ac1f383] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: The append-only event log does not require idempotence in the RFC 9110 sense at all. RFC 9110 idempotence is a property of HTTP request methods defined over 'intended effect on the server,' which is a semantic, declarative notion. The event log requires operational deduplication — a mechanical guarantee that duplicate physical writes are collapsed — which is a distinct and stronger property. Conflating the two obscures the design problem.\nMECHANISM: RFC 9110 idempotence says 'the intended effect of N identical requests equals the effect of 1.' This is a claim about the method's defined semantics, not a protocol-level mechanism. An event-log writer needs a concrete deduplication mechanism (e.g., monotonically increasing sequence numbers, content-addressed hashing, or client-assigned idempotency keys) to detect and suppress duplicates. This is an op", "handle": "ART_6", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
{"excerpt": "[cded8b977c50] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: RFC 9110 establishes that safe methods are a proper subset of idempotent methods: a safe method produces no side effects, so repeating it trivially yields the same (null) effect, satisfying idempotence. The converse fails: PUT and DELETE are idempotent but not safe. An append-only event log with at-least-once delivery requires idempotence — the weaker, more general property — because writers must mutate state (violating safety) but must not duplicate events under retry (requiring idempotence). Requiring safety would be self-contradictory for a write operation.\nMECHANISM: The subset relation follows from the definitions: safety means 'no intended state change' (RFC 9110 §9.2.1), and idempotence means 'same intended effect for N requests as for 1' (§9.2.2). If the effect of one request is 'no state change,' then the effect of N requests is also 'no ", "handle": "ART_7", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
{"excerpt": "[8e394f49e7f0] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: The critical distinction is not merely 'safe = read-only, idempotent = convergent under repetition,' but rather that RFC 9110 safety carries a client-expectation dimension: a safe method is one where 'the client does not request, and does not expect, any state change.' Idempotence carries no such expectation — the client expects a state change but expects it to be invariant under duplication. For the event log, this client-expectation dimension is decisive: writers always expect and request a state change (append), so safety is categorically impossible regardless of deduplication strategy. The log requires idempotence because writers expect mutation but must tolerate delivery duplication.\nMECHANISM: RFC 9110 §9.2.1 defines safety as not merely 'no side effect' but as a contract involving client intent: the client does not expect state change. This", "handle": "ART_8", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
