{"excerpt": "[519095e5cfa6] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: The distinction between safe and idempotent in RFC 9110 is fundamentally about the presence of side effects: safe methods have none (they are read-only operations that do not modify any server resource), while idempotent methods may have side effects but guarantee that the side effects of repeated identical requests equal those of a single request. For an append-only event log, the required property is idempotence. The log cannot require safety because appending is inherently a mutation. But it must require idempotence because at-least-once delivery means the writer's append request may be received and processed multiple times, and the log must remain correct — containing each logical event exactly once regardless of how many times the delivery layer retries.\nMECHANISM: Safe = no side effects (read-only). Idempotent = side effects allowed but inva", "handle": "ART_10", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
